2026-02-11 10:51:26,102 - DocumentGraderNode - INFO: Retrieved document How Does a Zero-Coupon Bond Differ From a Regular Bond?

Payment of interest or coupons is the key difference between a zero-coupon and a regular bond.

Regular bonds are also called coupon bonds. They pay interest over the life of the bond and then repay the principal at maturity.

A zero-coupon bond doesn't pay interest but instead trades at a deep discount. This gives investors a profit at maturity when they redeem the bond for its full face value. is relevant
